  "question": {
    "text": "The angular momentum for the electron in Bohr&rsquo;s orbit is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math>. If the electron is assumed to revolve in second orbit of hydrogen atom, then the change in angular momentum will be"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "Zero",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Bohr&#39;s formula for angular momentum&nbsp;of an electron in an orbit&nbsp;is given by&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>For the first orbit,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math>&nbsp;and the angular momentum is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>h</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></math></p><p>For&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>nd</mi></msup></math>&nbsp;orbit</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi></math></p><p>Hence, the change in angular momentum is&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#916;</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>L</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}